# Overlay and dialog contract

Apply these rules to Modal, Drawer, Overlay and the primitives they share.

## Test-first requirement

- Start every overlay regression with a failing test before implementation. Browser-visible regressions must not be closed by source-string assertions alone.
- A green jsdom suite is not sufficient evidence for focus, pointer, geometry, portal/theme inheritance or stacking behavior. Add browser-level coverage whenever the contract depends on real layout or browser focus.

## Portal and theme inheritance

- Components rendered through Teleport/Portal must continue to receive the active doctui theme. Never assume CSS custom properties placed only on the provider DOM ancestor will survive a teleport to `body`.
- Overlay roots must carry the resolved theme variables needed by teleported descendants, including semantic colors, spacing, radius, shadows and z-index tokens.
- Modal and Drawer must use one portal path. Do not wrap a component in Portal when its shared Overlay already portals the layer.

## Dismissal stack

- Escape and outside-pointer dismissal must be coordinated through the shared dismissable-layer stack. Only the top active layer may dismiss in response to a global interaction.
- `closeOnEscape` and `closeOnClickOutside` are independent public behaviors. Wire the matching callback as well as the flag; a boolean prop that never reaches a dismissal handler is incomplete.
- Use one outside-dismissal source per interaction. Do not wire both document `pointerdown` dismissal and backdrop `click` dismissal for the same Modal/Drawer interaction.
- Nested overlay tests must verify that the child closes first and the parent remains open.

## Focus

- Modal-like surfaces must trap focus while active and restore focus to the element that opened that specific layer when they close.
- Focus containment must be stack-aware: only the top focus trap may redirect focus or cycle Tab/Shift+Tab.
- Nested layers restore focus one level at a time: child trigger first, then parent trigger when the parent closes.
- Clicking or programmatically focusing outside the top dialog must not permanently escape an enabled trap.

## Scroll locking

- Scroll locking is reference-counted globally but acquisition/release ownership is per hook instance.
- One instance may acquire at most once and release at most once. A false transition followed by unmount must not decrement twice.
- Preserve the body's original inline overflow value when the first lock starts and restore it only after the final lock releases.
- Public `lockScroll` behavior must be observable; disabling it must not mutate body overflow.

## Accessible naming

- Every element with `role="dialog"` must have an accessible name.
- A visible `title` should generate `aria-labelledby` and takes precedence over `ariaLabel`.
- `ariaLabel` is the explicit titleless-dialog path. Safety fallbacks may prevent unnamed dialogs but must not replace documenting the explicit naming paths.

## Visual and theme contract

- Public `size`, `position`, `radius`, `centered`, `color`, and `opacity` props must map to observable geometry/state or semantic theme variables.
- Backdrop opacity must affect only the backdrop color, never the overlay's child content.
- `withOverlay=false` must disable the visual backdrop without breaking the layer, focus or dismissal contract.
- `overlayProps` must be forwarded to the shared Overlay rather than copied into unrelated surface attributes.
- Modal and Drawer size scales must cover `xs`, `sm`, `md`, `lg`, and `xl` with real width changes. If custom CSS sizes are accepted, map them directly to the public width variable.
- Overlay-family layering must use `--dui-z-index-*` theme variables. Do not add literal z-index numbers in component styles.

## Storybook Controls

- Every public Overlay, Modal and Drawer prop that can be represented safely in Storybook must have a functional Control in a dedicated component story.
- Each component story meta must declare `component`, `args`, and `argTypes`; the primary story must render from `args` instead of hard-coding the props being documented.
- Keep basic playground stories separate from advanced compositions. Advanced stories must include realistic nested Modal → Drawer behavior.
- A Storybook story that only provides custom launcher buttons does not satisfy Controls coverage for public props.

## Tests and documentation

- Cover Escape enabled/disabled, outside click enabled/disabled, nested ordering, focus containment/restoration, reference-counted scroll lock, accessible names, sizing, backdrop tokens, portal/theme inheritance and z-index tokens.
- Storybook must include interaction-oriented states and at least one realistic nested composition when multiple overlay surfaces interact.
- VitePress and metadata must document the same naming, dismissal, focus, scroll-lock, sizing, backdrop and layering contract.
